THE GIVEN CODE DOES NOT SEEM TO HAVE ANY SYNTAX ERRORS OR INCORRECT USAGE OF THE FADEIN() FUNCTION PER SE. ALL USAGES MATCH THE FADEIN()'S PARAMETERS DOCUMENTATION, WHERE IT TAKES `'FAST'`, `'SLOW'` OR AN INTEGER REPRESENTING MILLISECONDS AS INPUT.

HOWEVER:
"$("#DIV4").FADEIN(-200);" LOOKS ODD BECAUSE A NEGATIVE DURATION FOR THE FADEIN() FUNCTION MIGHT NOT MAKE SENSE AND IT'S USUALLY CONSIDERED AS A PROGRAMMING MISTAKE. IF A NEGATIVE NUMBER OR ZERO IS GIVEN, JQUERY TREATS IT AS IF IT WAS ZERO AND COMPLETES THE ANIMATION INSTANTLY.

TO IMPROVE THE CODE, RATHER THAN USING MULTIPLE INDIVIDUAL ID SELECTORS WHICH CAN BE SLOW, YOU MIGHT WANT TO USE A COMMON CLASS SELECTOR OR STORE THEM IN VARIABLES IF THESE ELEMENTS ARE RE-USED ELSEWHERE IN CODE. HERE IS THE REFACTORED CODE:

