HERE IS A JAVASCRIPT FUNCTION THAT PERFORMS THE REQUIRED OPERATIONS:

```JAVASCRIPT
FUNCTION MERGESORTUNIQUE(...ARRAYS) {
    LET MERGED = [].CONCAT(...ARRAYS);
    LET RESULT = [];

    // BUBBLE SORT
    FOR (LET I = 0; I < MERGED.LENGTH; I++) {
        FOR (LET J = 0; J < MERGED.LENGTH - I - 1; J++) {
            IF (MERGED[J] > MERGED[J + 1]) {
                LET TEMP = MERGED[J];
                MERGED[J] = MERGED[J + 1];
                MERGED[J + 1] = TEMP;
            }
        }
    }

    // REMOVE DUPLICATES AND CREATE THE RESULT ARRAY
    FOR (LET I = 0; I < MERGED.LENGTH; I++) {
        IF (MERGED[I] !== MERGED[I + 1]) {
            RESULT.PUSH(MERGED[I]);
        }
    }

    RETURN RESULT;
}

LET A = [1, 2, 3];
LET B = [4, 5, 3];
LET C = [2, 6, 7];

CONSOLE.LOG(MERGESORTUNIQUE(A, B, C)); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
```

THIS FUNCTION USES THE SPREAD OPERATOR (I.E., '...') TO TAKE MULTIPLE ARRAYS AS ARGUMENTS. IT BEGINS BY MERGING ALL GIVEN ARRAYS INTO ONE BIG ARRAY USING 'CONCAT'. THE MERGED ARRAY IS THEN SORTED USING A BUBBLE SORT ALGORITHM. AFTER THAT, ANY DUPLICATE VALUES ARE REMOVED WHILE CONSTRUCTING THE FINAL RESULT ARRAY. ELEMENTS ARE ONLY ADDED TO THE RESULT ARRAY IF THEY ARE NOT THE SAME AS THE SUBSEQUENT ELEMENT IN THE MERGED ARRAY, WHICH EFFECTIVELY LEAVES ONLY UNIQUE VALUES IN THE RESULT. 

THE 'CONSOLE.LOG' AT THE BOTTOM OF THIS SCRIPT RUNS AN EXAMPLE WITH YOUR GIVEN INPUT ARRAYS. AS EXPECTED, IT LOGS THE ARRAY [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7], WHICH IS THE SORTED, MERGED, AND DE-DUPLICATED ARRAY.

