THE METROPOLIS-HASTINGS ALGORITHM IS A MARKOV CHAIN MONTE CARLO (MCMC) METHOD USED FOR OBTAINING A SEQUENCE OF RANDOM SAMPLES FROM PROBABILITY DISTRIBUTIONS, ESPECIALLY WHEN THE NUMBER OF DIMENSIONS ARE TOO HIGH TO SOLVE THE PROBLEM ANALYTICALLY.

HERE'S AN EXAMPLE OF A FUNCTION IN R LANGUAGE TO GENERATE A RANDOM NUMBER SEQUENCE BETWEEN 1 AND 20 USING THIS ALGORITHM:

```R
METROPOLIS_HASTINGS <- FUNCTION(DIST, LEN = 10000, START = 10, PROPOSAL_SD = 0.5) {
    # INITIALIZES THE SEQUENCE WITH THE START VALUE
    X <- NUMERIC(LEN)
    X[1] <- START

    FOR (I IN 2:LEN) {
        # GENERATES A PROPOSAL VALUE
        Y <- RNORM(1, MEAN = X[I-1], SD = PROPOSAL_SD)
        # COMPUTES THE ACCEPTANCE PROBABILITY
        ALPHA <- MIN(1, DIST(Y[1]) / DIST(X[I-1]))

        # MIGHT ACCEPT OR REJECT THE PROPOSAL
        IF (RUNIF(1) < ALPHA) {
            X[I] <- Y
        } ELSE {
            X[I] <- X[I-1]
        }
    }

    RETURN(X)
}

# DEFINE THE UNIFORM DISTRIBUTION PROBABILITY DENSITY FUNCTION
UNIFORM_PDF <- FUNCTION(X) {
    IF (X > 20 || X < 1) {
        RETURN(0)
    } ELSE {
        RETURN(1/20)
    }
}

# GENERATE A SEQUENCE USING METROPOLIS-HASTINGS ALGORITHM
SEQUENCE <- METROPOLIS_HASTINGS(UNIFORM_PDF)
```

IN THIS SAMPLE, THE STARTING POINT OF THE SEQUENCE IS SET AS 10 AND THE STANDARD DEVIATION OF THE PROPOSAL DISTRIBUTION IS SET TO 0.5 FOR GENERATING PROPOSAL VALUES. NOTE THAT THE METROPOLIS-HASTINGS ALGORITHM IS BASED ON A MARKOV CHAIN, WHERE EACH VALUE IN THE SEQUENCE ONLY DEPENDS ON THE PREVIOUS ONE. IN THIS CASE, THE ALGORITHM GENERATES A SEQUENCE FROM A UNIFORM DISTRIBUTION BETWEEN 1 AND 20, WHICH MIGHT NOT BE THE MOST ILLUSTRATIVE EXAMPLE BECAUSE YOU COULD DIRECTLY GENERATE RANDOM NUMBERS FROM A UNIFORM DISTRIBUTION. HOWEVER, IT HELPS UNDERSTAND HOW THE METROPOLIS-HASTINGS ALGORITHM WORKS.

