THIS PROBLEM SEEMS LIKE IT CAN BE SOLVED MORE EFFICIENTLY BY USING A TWO POINTER TECHNIQUE. WE WILL KEEP TRACK OF EACH UNIQUE CAR PAIR AND THEIR COLLISION COUNT IN A HASH MAP IN C++, WHICH IS UNORDERED_MAP. WE WILL ALSO KEEP TRACK OF TOTAL COLLISION COUNT. HERE IS AN OPTIMIZED VERSION OF THE CODE IN C++:

```CPP
#INCLUDE<BITS/STDC++.H>
USING NAMESPACE STD;

INT CAR_RACE_COLLISION(VECTOR<INT> LEFTWARD, VECTOR<INT> RIGHTWARD, UNORDERED_MAP<INT, INT> SPEEDVARIATIONS){
    SORT(LEFTWARD.BEGIN(), LEFTWARD.END());
    SORT(RIGHTWARD.BEGIN(), RIGHTWARD.END());

    UNORDERED_MAP<STRING, INT> COLLISIONPAIRS;
    INT TOTALCOLLISIONS = 0;

    INT I = 0, J = RIGHTWARD.SIZE() - 1;
    WHILE(I < LEFTWARD.SIZE() && J >= 0){
        IF(LEFTWARD[I] + SPEEDVARIATIONS[LEFTWARD[I]] < RIGHTWARD[J] + SPEEDVARIATIONS[RIGHTWARD[J]]){
            STRING PAIR_KEY = TO_STRING(LEFTWARD[I]) + " - " + TO_STRING(RIGHTWARD[J]);
            COLLISIONPAIRS[PAIR_KEY]++;
            TOTALCOLLISIONS++;
            I++;
        }
        ELSE J--;
    }
    COUT << "TOTAL COLLISIONS: " << TOTALCOLLISIONS << "\N";
    COUT << "UNIQUE COLLISION COUNTS: " << COLLISIONPAIRS.SIZE() << "\N";
    FOR(AUTO &P: COLLISIONPAIRS){
        COUT << "CAR PAIR " << P.FIRST << ": " << P.SECOND << " TIMES\N";
    }
}
```

IN THIS VERSION, LEFTWARD AND RIGHTWARD ARE VECTORS THAT REPRESENT THE RIGHTWARD MOVING AND LEFTWARD MOVING CAR'S SPEEDS AND SPEEDVARIATIONS IS A HASH MAP THAT REPRESENTS THE SPEED VARIATIONS FOR EACH CAR.

THE TIME COMPLEXITY OF THIS FUNCTION IS O(N LOG N + M LOG M) WHERE 'N' IS THE LENGTH OF THE LEFTWARDS VECTOR AND 'M' IS THE LENGTH OF THE RIGHTWARDS VECTOR. THIS IS BECAUSE WE SORT BOTH THE VECTORS BEFORE TRAVERSING THEM WHICH TAKES N LOG N AND M LOG M TIME RESPECTIVELY. THE WHILE LOOP THEN TAKES MAX(N, M) TIME WHICH DOES NOT AFFECT THE OVERALL TIME COMPLEXITY. THIS IS A SIGNIFICANT IMPROVEMENT OVER THE PREVIOUS VERSION WHICH PRESUMABLY HAD A TIME COMPLEXITY OF O(N * M).

