```javascript
const multiArr = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]];

let evenNumbers = multiArr.reduce((accumulator, subArray) => {
    let evenNumsInSubArray = subArray.filter(num => num % 2 === 0);
    return accumulator.concat(evenNumsInSubArray);
}, []);

evenNumbers.sort((a, b) => a - b);

console.log(evenNumbers);
```
When the above code is run with the provided array, it will output `[2, 4, 6, 8]`, which are the even numbers from each subarray, sorted in ascending order.

Please take note that JavaScript's sort method does not sort numbers correctly. It converts them to strings and sorts them lexicographically. So, we need to provide a callback function `(a, b) => a - b` to it. 

Empty subarrays are handled by the fact that filtering an empty array will result in an empty array and will thus not affect the final results. 

The `%` (modulus) operator work with negatives numbers too as `-4 % 2` is `0` and `-3 % 2` is `-1`. Thus, negative even numbers would be correctly identified as well.

